1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,637
Your CTO has two questions. "The same
commit runs on every laptop and fails in CI

2
00:00:09,677 --> 00:00:12,321
with a missing module. What is different?"

3
00:00:12,661 --> 00:00:17,676
And the second. "One cleanup command deleted
two days of labelled evaluation data.

4
00:00:17,716 --> 00:00:19,094
Can Git bring it back?"

5
00:00:21,434 --> 00:00:26,641
The answer to the first: the laptops have
a case-insensitive filesystem, one where

6
00:00:26,681 --> 00:00:31,760
upper and lower case count as the same
name, and the CI runner, the machine that

7
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:37,135
tests every change, does not. The answer to
the second: git clean -fdx deletes files

8
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:39,438
that Git never had a copy of. So no.

9
00:00:39,778 --> 00:00:43,753
Both failures happen at the border
between Git and the filesystem.

10
00:00:43,793 --> 00:00:48,801
Git has a small, exact idea of what a file
is. Your filesystem has a different one.

11
00:00:48,841 --> 00:00:53,607
Today we walk that border: permissions,
directories, upper and lower case, line

12
00:00:53,647 --> 00:00:58,169
endings, and the one everyday command
that removes data Git has no copy of.

13
00:00:58,209 --> 00:01:01,210
Keep that "no" in mind.
One command will prove it.

14
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:06,722
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:06,762 --> 00:01:11,589
This is the last video on the working
tree, the folder of files you edit, and it

16
00:01:11,629 --> 00:01:16,578
collects five topics that have the same
shape. In each, the question is: what does

17
00:01:16,618 --> 00:01:21,446
Git record, what does the filesystem do,
and what happens when the two disagree?

18
00:01:21,786 --> 00:01:26,497
The first four are quick. The fifth, git
clean, carries a red label, and before I

19
00:01:26,537 --> 00:01:30,661
run it I'll answer the five questions
for a dangerous command out loud.

20
00:01:32,501 --> 00:01:34,080
Here's what you'll walk away with.

21
00:01:34,420 --> 00:01:35,832
After this video you can:

22
00:01:36,172 --> 00:01:40,893
State what Git records about file permissions,
and set the executable bit in the

23
00:01:40,933 --> 00:01:41,250
index.

24
00:01:41,590 --> 00:01:44,336
Explain why Git cannot
track an empty directory.

25
00:01:44,676 --> 00:01:48,836
Reproduce and repair a case-only rename
on a case-insensitive filesystem.

26
00:01:49,176 --> 00:01:52,912
Preview and run git clean with the
narrowest scope that does the job.

27
00:01:54,752 --> 00:01:59,641
File modes and symbolic links. In one
sentence: besides its content, Git records one

28
00:01:59,681 --> 00:02:03,396
more thing about a file: its type,
and whether it is executable.

29
00:02:03,736 --> 00:02:07,740
Precisely. An index entry and
a tree entry carry a mode.

30
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:13,734
The index is Git's list of what goes into
the next commit, and a tree is its record

31
00:02:13,774 --> 00:02:19,078
of one directory. For files there are
three modes. 100644, a regular file.

32
00:02:19,118 --> 00:02:25,144
100755, an executable file. And 120000, a
symbolic link, a small file that points at

33
00:02:25,184 --> 00:02:31,065
another path. The User's Manual puts it
bluntly: "Git actually only pays attention

34
00:02:31,105 --> 00:02:36,987
to the executable bit." Owner, group, the
other permission bits and timestamps are

35
00:02:37,027 --> 00:02:37,926
not recorded.

36
00:02:38,266 --> 00:02:44,159
A mode change is a change: status reports
it, and the diff shows old mode and new

37
00:02:44,199 --> 00:02:50,165
mode with no content lines. The mode isn't
part of the blob, the object that holds

38
00:02:50,205 --> 00:02:56,026
the file's content. When the filesystem
can't express the bit, you set it in the

39
00:02:56,066 --> 00:03:01,226
index directly, with git add --chmod=+x
or git update-index --chmod=+x.

40
00:03:01,566 --> 00:03:05,123
Both are 🟢 SAFE: they change
the mode in the index entry.

41
00:03:05,163 --> 00:03:08,530
With core.fileMode=false,
Git stops comparing the bit.

42
00:03:08,570 --> 00:03:13,514
That's what git init and git clone configure
when they detect a filesystem that

43
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:14,523
doesn't keep it.

44
00:03:14,863 --> 00:03:18,886
A symbolic link is stored as a blob
whose content is the link text.

45
00:03:18,926 --> 00:03:23,859
Git never follows the link, and never stores
the target's content under the link's

46
00:03:23,899 --> 00:03:24,162
path.

47
00:03:24,502 --> 00:03:27,811
Empty directories. In one
sentence: Git tracks files.

48
00:03:27,851 --> 00:03:32,486
A directory exists in a commit only
because a file path passes through it.

49
00:03:32,826 --> 00:03:37,758
The index is a list of files, so there's
nothing to add for an empty directory.

50
00:03:37,798 --> 00:03:42,793
The convention is to give the directory
a file. A .gitignore inside it, with the

51
00:03:42,833 --> 00:03:47,576
pattern * and the exception !.gitignore,
does two jobs at once: it keeps the

52
00:03:47,616 --> 00:03:50,849
directory in the repository
and ignores its content.

53
00:03:50,889 --> 00:03:55,758
You'll also meet empty files named
.gitkeep. The name means nothing to Git and

54
00:03:55,798 --> 00:04:01,045
appears nowhere in its documentation. The
rule works in the other direction too: git

55
00:04:01,085 --> 00:04:04,192
rm on the last tracked file
removes the directory.

56
00:04:04,532 --> 00:04:10,376
The case-insensitive filesystem. In one
sentence: Git treats Config.py and config.py

57
00:04:10,416 --> 00:04:16,051
as two different paths, the default macOS
filesystem treats them as one file, and

58
00:04:16,091 --> 00:04:21,515
the setting that reconciles the two,
core.ignoreCase, hides case-only renames.

59
00:04:21,855 --> 00:04:27,573
Precisely. Git stores file names as byte
sequences and performs no case folding, so

60
00:04:27,613 --> 00:04:33,330
it never treats upper and lower case as
equal. When git init or git clone creates a

61
00:04:33,370 --> 00:04:38,810
repository, it probes the filesystem and
sets core.ignoreCase=true if names are

62
00:04:38,850 --> 00:04:42,626
case-insensitive, as they are
on a default APFS volume.

63
00:04:42,966 --> 00:04:47,608
The manual describes the effect: if a
directory listing finds makefile when Git

64
00:04:47,648 --> 00:04:52,172
expects Makefile, Git will assume it is
really the same file, and continue to

65
00:04:52,212 --> 00:04:57,152
remember it as Makefile. The manual also
calls the variable internal, and warns that

66
00:04:57,192 --> 00:05:00,352
changing it by hand may result
in unexpected behavior.

67
00:05:00,692 --> 00:05:04,158
The transcripts in this segment
assume a case-insensitive volume.

68
00:05:04,198 --> 00:05:08,311
On a case-sensitive one they come out
differently, and that difference is the

69
00:05:08,351 --> 00:05:08,689
lesson.

70
00:05:09,029 --> 00:05:14,204
The trap has two forms. In the first, you
rename a file with mv, changing only the

71
00:05:14,244 --> 00:05:19,292
case. On a case-insensitive filesystem
such a rename never reaches the index, so

72
00:05:19,332 --> 00:05:24,570
it's never committed. In the second, someone
on Linux commits two files whose names

73
00:05:24,610 --> 00:05:29,340
differ only by case, which is legal
there. On a Mac both names can't exist.

74
00:05:29,680 --> 00:05:33,190
Line endings, in brief. Git
stores the bytes it is given.

75
00:05:33,230 --> 00:05:38,421
A file saved with Windows line endings,
CRLF, which is a carriage return plus a line

76
00:05:38,461 --> 00:05:43,590
feed, is stored with them unless an attribute
or a configuration value tells Git to

77
00:05:43,630 --> 00:05:48,635
convert. When an editor on another platform
rewrites every line ending, the whole

78
00:05:48,675 --> 00:05:50,129
file becomes one change.

79
00:05:50,469 --> 00:05:55,433
The durable fix is a committed .gitattributes
with * text=auto and one git add

80
00:05:55,473 --> 00:06:00,757
--renormalize ., so that the repository
holds LF, the line feed alone, for everyone

81
00:06:00,797 --> 00:06:05,569
regardless of personal settings. A later
chapter covers attributes in full.

82
00:06:05,609 --> 00:06:08,391
Here you only need to
recognize the symptom.

83
00:06:08,731 --> 00:06:13,873
git clean. In one sentence: git clean deletes
untracked files from the working tree,

84
00:06:13,913 --> 00:06:18,623
which makes it the one everyday command
that removes data Git has no copy of.

85
00:06:18,663 --> 00:06:21,831
An untracked file is one
with no entry in the index.

86
00:06:21,871 --> 00:06:22,941
It is 🔴 DANGEROUS.

87
00:06:23,281 --> 00:06:27,945
By default it removes untracked files that
are not ignored, in the current directory

88
00:06:27,985 --> 00:06:30,801
and below, and doesn't enter
untracked directories.

89
00:06:30,841 --> 00:06:32,088
The options widen that.

90
00:06:32,428 --> 00:06:36,781
-n, or --dry-run: show what would
be removed, and remove nothing.

91
00:06:36,821 --> 00:06:41,985
-f: required to delete anything, because
clean.requireForce defaults to true.

92
00:06:42,025 --> 00:06:47,256
A second -f allows deleting untracked
directories that contain their own .git.

93
00:06:47,296 --> 00:06:52,865
-d: also remove untracked directories.
Capital -X: remove only ignored files, which

94
00:06:52,905 --> 00:06:57,393
means build products and caches,
keeping files you created by hand.

95
00:06:57,433 --> 00:07:02,800
Small -x: do not use ignore rules at all,
and remove untracked and ignored files

96
00:07:02,840 --> 00:07:06,990
alike. -e with a pattern: add an
exclude pattern for this run.

97
00:07:07,030 --> 00:07:08,409
-i: interactive mode.

98
00:07:08,749 --> 00:07:13,171
-x and -X differ by one keystroke,
and by which of your files survive.

99
00:07:13,511 --> 00:07:19,108
Quick quiz, three options. An ignored
.env file holds your only copy of some

100
00:07:19,148 --> 00:07:23,929
settings. Which option removes it:
capital -X, small -x, or both?

101
00:07:23,969 --> 00:07:25,116
Say your answer.

102
00:07:29,956 --> 00:07:34,297
Both. Capital X removes only
ignored files, and .env is ignored.

103
00:07:34,337 --> 00:07:39,773
Small x removes ignored and untracked
alike. If you said small x only, you're in

104
00:07:39,813 --> 00:07:40,663
good company.

105
00:07:41,003 --> 00:07:46,017
Inside .git, nothing changes. Clean
reads the index to learn which paths are

106
00:07:46,057 --> 00:07:49,409
tracked, and the ignore
rules to classify the rest.

107
00:07:49,449 --> 00:07:54,397
It writes no object, moves no ref, and
leaves no record of what it deleted.

108
00:07:56,237 --> 00:07:59,416
Keep the workbench and the
parts cabinet from video 11.

109
00:07:59,456 --> 00:08:03,922
For git clean, the textbook's analogy
is sweeping the workbench into the bin.

110
00:08:03,962 --> 00:08:08,721
Everything that came out of the cabinet is
safe, because the cabinet still has it.

111
00:08:08,761 --> 00:08:13,462
In the picture, that's a tracked file: the
index and HEAD, the last commit, still

112
00:08:13,502 --> 00:08:13,930
hold it.

113
00:08:14,270 --> 00:08:18,902
Everything else is gone, and Git can't
tell a wood shaving from the prototype you

114
00:08:18,942 --> 00:08:22,304
spent a week on. The textbook
says this analogy holds well.

115
00:08:22,344 --> 00:08:25,996
Its only weakness is that a real
bin can be searched afterwards.

116
00:08:26,336 --> 00:08:30,921
For the other four topics, one sentence
covers them: Git's idea of a file is

117
00:08:30,961 --> 00:08:35,851
content, a path as bytes, and one of three
modes. Everything else your filesystem

118
00:08:35,891 --> 00:08:40,781
knows is outside that idea: owners, other
permission bits, empty directories, and

119
00:08:40,821 --> 00:08:45,650
whether upper and lower case are the same.
Trouble starts where the filesystem's

120
00:08:45,690 --> 00:08:46,502
idea leaks in.

121
00:08:48,342 --> 00:08:52,010
That's the first form of the trap.
Read the line marked root cause.

122
00:08:52,350 --> 00:08:56,399
And the second form. Two entries
in the tree, two different blobs.

123
00:08:56,439 --> 00:09:01,479
The filesystem has one slot for both names.
Whichever is written second overwrites

124
00:09:01,519 --> 00:09:05,382
the first, and the other path is
reported as modified for ever.

125
00:09:09,722 --> 00:09:15,692
A mode change is a change: status shows it,
and the diff has no content lines, only

126
00:09:15,732 --> 00:09:21,774
old mode and new mode. After git add, the
entry has mode 100755 and the same blob ID

127
00:09:21,814 --> 00:09:22,498
as before.

128
00:09:22,838 --> 00:09:27,728
Other permission bits are not recorded at
all: chmod 600 on a file leaves status

129
00:09:27,768 --> 00:09:28,344
unchanged.

130
00:09:28,684 --> 00:09:34,012
git add --chmod=+x 🟢 SAFE sets the bit in
the index without touching the file on

131
00:09:34,052 --> 00:09:39,513
disk. Afterwards the index says executable
and the disk doesn't, so status reports

132
00:09:39,553 --> 00:09:42,801
the file as modified in
the working tree as well.

133
00:09:43,141 --> 00:09:48,631
A symbolic link: mode 120000, and a blob
that holds the link text, not the content

134
00:09:48,671 --> 00:09:49,576
of the target.

135
00:09:49,916 --> 00:09:54,993
Two empty directories are created. Predict
what git status and git add data say.

136
00:09:55,033 --> 00:09:56,017
Say it out loud.

137
00:10:00,857 --> 00:10:06,086
Nothing, both times. A clean working
tree. git add data adds nothing and says

138
00:10:06,126 --> 00:10:09,371
nothing, and the index
has no entry under data/.

139
00:10:09,711 --> 00:10:14,435
The placeholder: a .gitignore that ignores
everything in the directory except

140
00:10:14,475 --> 00:10:14,868
itself.

141
00:10:15,208 --> 00:10:20,059
And the other direction: git rm 🟡 CAUTION
on the only tracked file in docs/ removes

142
00:10:20,099 --> 00:10:24,949
the directory with it. An untracked file
keeps the directory alive on disk, and Git

143
00:10:24,989 --> 00:10:26,952
still doesn't track the directory.

144
00:10:29,792 --> 00:10:34,109
git init probed the filesystem
and set core.ignoreCase to true.

145
00:10:34,449 --> 00:10:40,731
Now rename Config.py to config.py with
plain mv. Predict what git status reports.

146
00:10:40,771 --> 00:10:42,839
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

147
00:10:47,679 --> 00:10:53,272
Nothing at all. The file on disk is now
config.py. Status is clean, git add -A

148
00:10:53,312 --> 00:10:57,317
records nothing, and the index
still says src/Config.py.

149
00:10:57,357 --> 00:11:03,383
Every commit you make will keep the old
name, and every Linux machine will check out

150
00:11:03,423 --> 00:11:07,211
the old name. Almost everyone
is caught by this once.

151
00:11:07,551 --> 00:11:11,929
The rename has to be made in the
index, and git mv 🟢 SAFE does that.

152
00:11:12,269 --> 00:11:17,495
The mirror image. A teammate on Linux
commits two files whose names differ only by

153
00:11:17,535 --> 00:11:22,826
case. A Mac can't hold both on disk, so the
script imitates the teammate by writing

154
00:11:22,866 --> 00:11:24,046
the index directly.

155
00:11:24,386 --> 00:11:30,570
Two entries, two blobs: 1e0b1ad and 5fac60c.
Predict what a clone on a Mac does.

156
00:11:30,610 --> 00:11:34,460
One file, two files, or an
error? Say it out loud.

157
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:46,849
One file, and a warning. Git warns during
the clone that paths have collided.

158
00:11:46,889 --> 00:11:51,676
One file lands on disk, and the other path
is reported as modified for ever: when

159
00:11:51,716 --> 00:11:54,835
Git reads one name, it gets
the content of the other.

160
00:11:54,875 --> 00:11:59,364
No git restore can fix it, because
writing either file overwrites the other.

161
00:11:59,704 --> 00:12:04,553
The detector folds every tracked path
to lower case and looks for duplicates.

162
00:12:04,593 --> 00:12:09,506
The documented fix is to stop tracking
one of the names with git rm --cached 🟡

163
00:12:09,546 --> 00:12:12,744
CAUTION, commit, and check
the other one out again.

164
00:12:12,784 --> 00:12:17,951
The detector is cheap enough to run in CI
on every pull request, which is a change

165
00:12:17,991 --> 00:12:19,221
proposed for review.

166
00:12:22,061 --> 00:12:26,896
git ls-files --eol shows line endings.
i/ is the content in the index, w/ the

167
00:12:26,936 --> 00:12:30,821
content in the working tree, and
attr/ the attribute in force.

168
00:12:31,161 --> 00:12:36,462
An editor saved the file with CRLF. Three
lines "changed", although no character you

169
00:12:36,502 --> 00:12:41,294
can see did, and --ignore-cr-at-eol
confirms that the only difference is the

170
00:12:41,334 --> 00:12:43,837
carriage return at the end of each line.

171
00:12:46,677 --> 00:12:50,314
The starting point: three untracked
paths and four ignored ones.

172
00:12:50,354 --> 00:12:54,565
Two question marks mean untracked, and
two exclamation marks mean ignored.

173
00:12:54,905 --> 00:13:00,397
Without -f, -n or -i, Git refuses to
run. Always start with the dry runs.

174
00:13:00,437 --> 00:13:03,504
git clean -n is 🟢 SAFE: it's the preview.

175
00:13:03,844 --> 00:13:09,016
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Go
back to the seven paths of the starting

176
00:13:09,056 --> 00:13:12,991
point. Write down which of them
git clean -n -d would remove.

177
00:13:13,031 --> 00:13:15,076
Pause me, and write your answer.

178
00:13:22,916 --> 00:13:26,317
With -d, two paths go:
experiments/ and scratch.py.

179
00:13:26,357 --> 00:13:29,826
If you also wrote vendor,
that's the natural answer.

180
00:13:29,866 --> 00:13:35,359
The directory vendor/tokenizer is a repository
of its own, and is skipped by every

181
00:13:35,399 --> 00:13:40,689
run with fewer than two -f. Each added
option widens the list, and the last one

182
00:13:40,729 --> 00:13:42,309
includes .env and data/.

183
00:13:42,649 --> 00:13:45,486
-e protects one pattern from a -x run.

184
00:13:45,826 --> 00:13:51,231
What it changes: the working tree only.
What it can destroy: untracked files.

185
00:13:51,271 --> 00:13:57,172
With -d, untracked directories. With -x,
also ignored files such as .env, downloaded

186
00:13:57,212 --> 00:14:02,122
datasets, model checkpoints, virtual
environments and local databases.

187
00:14:02,462 --> 00:14:06,182
How to preview: the same
command with -n in place of -f.

188
00:14:06,222 --> 00:14:10,412
Read every line of the output.
How to recover: not through Git.

189
00:14:10,452 --> 00:14:13,366
Backups, or editor
history for single files.

190
00:14:13,706 --> 00:14:18,933
When it is appropriate: to prove a build
from a pristine tree, to reset a CI

191
00:14:18,973 --> 00:14:24,617
workspace, to clear generated files that
the build tool's own clean target misses.

192
00:14:24,657 --> 00:14:30,300
Prefer -X to -x, pass a pathspec to narrow
the blast radius, and protect what must

193
00:14:30,340 --> 00:14:31,409
survive with -e.

194
00:14:31,749 --> 00:14:37,139
git clean -f -d: the untracked file and
directory are removed, the nested repository

195
00:14:37,179 --> 00:14:40,371
is skipped, and the ignored
paths are still there.

196
00:14:40,711 --> 00:14:45,110
git clean -f -d -x: .env, data/,
the log and the cache are removed.

197
00:14:45,150 --> 00:14:50,674
And git fsck prints nothing. There's no
dangling blob to rescue, no leftover object,

198
00:14:50,714 --> 00:14:56,239
because none of these files was ever added.
That's the whole difference between this

199
00:14:56,279 --> 00:15:01,538
command and the losses of the last video.
That's the proof I promised: Git can't

200
00:15:01,578 --> 00:15:02,466
bring it back.

201
00:15:02,806 --> 00:15:06,616
One -f never deletes a directory
that has its own .git.

202
00:15:06,656 --> 00:15:09,836
Two do. The script shows
it as a dry run only.

203
00:15:11,676 --> 00:15:13,324
Five mistakes to watch for.

204
00:15:13,664 --> 00:15:19,442
A script fails in CI with "Permission
denied". Root cause: it was committed with

205
00:15:19,482 --> 00:15:25,478
mode 100644; git ls-files --stage <path>
shows it, and the fix is to set the bit in

206
00:15:25,518 --> 00:15:27,005
the index and commit.

207
00:15:27,345 --> 00:15:32,288
An expected directory is missing after a
clone. Root cause: Git does not track empty

208
00:15:32,328 --> 00:15:37,212
directories; commit a placeholder or, better,
have the code create the directory it

209
00:15:37,252 --> 00:15:37,805
writes to.

210
00:15:38,145 --> 00:15:43,905
A renamed file still has its old name on
Linux. Root cause: a case-only rename made

211
00:15:43,945 --> 00:15:48,168
with mv on macOS never reached
the index; rename with git mv.

212
00:15:48,508 --> 00:15:54,948
Every line of a file shows as changed.
Root cause: an editor rewrote the line

213
00:15:54,988 --> 00:15:58,819
endings; git ls-files
--eol shows i/lf w/crlf.

214
00:15:59,159 --> 00:16:04,876
git clean -fdx copied from an answer
deletes the only copies of ignored data.

215
00:16:04,916 --> 00:16:11,156
Root cause: -x disregards ignore rules, and
the files were never added, so no object

216
00:16:11,196 --> 00:16:13,025
holds them; run -n first.

217
00:16:14,865 --> 00:16:20,042
Now, out of the lab. The typical loss, as
the textbook describes it, is in an ML

218
00:16:20,082 --> 00:16:24,541
repository where data/, checkpoints/
and .env are ignored on purpose.

219
00:16:24,581 --> 00:16:30,018
Someone copies git clean -fdx from a "fix
your broken build" answer, and the ignored

220
00:16:30,058 --> 00:16:34,256
files, which were the only copies,
are deleted in under a second.

221
00:16:34,296 --> 00:16:39,668
The command did what its options say. The
defence is the habit of running -n first,

222
00:16:39,708 --> 00:16:44,754
and of keeping irreplaceable data outside
the working tree or in real storage.

223
00:16:45,094 --> 00:16:50,686
And the case trap is specific to teams that
develop on macOS or Windows and deploy

224
00:16:50,726 --> 00:16:54,257
on Linux, which describes
most backend and ML teams.

225
00:16:54,297 --> 00:16:59,751
Python imports, Java class files and Docker
COPY paths are all case-sensitive on

226
00:16:59,791 --> 00:17:00,164
Linux.

227
00:17:02,004 --> 00:17:08,628
Your turn. Do Exercise 2.1, Level 1, "every
short status code, made on purpose", in

228
00:17:08,668 --> 00:17:15,374
exercises/m01-m05-foundations.md, repeated
with an executable script and a case-only

229
00:17:15,414 --> 00:17:18,264
rename added to the states you make.

230
00:17:18,604 --> 00:17:22,251
Predict the status code for the
mode change before you look.

231
00:17:22,291 --> 00:17:27,413
For the case-only rename made with mv,
predict what git status and git ls-files will

232
00:17:27,453 --> 00:17:29,195
show, and only then run them.

233
00:17:31,035 --> 00:17:36,717
Q48: "A Python module imports fine on macOS
and fails on the Linux CI runner after

234
00:17:36,757 --> 00:17:42,508
someone renamed it. What happened inside
Git, and how do you prevent a recurrence?"

235
00:17:44,848 --> 00:17:49,568
Answer out loud. A strong answer says
what the index held before and after the

236
00:17:49,608 --> 00:17:54,693
rename, names the setting involved and who
set it, and explains why Git behaves this

237
00:17:54,733 --> 00:17:59,819
way by design. For prevention, give more
than one layer: a habit for the person, and

238
00:17:59,859 --> 00:18:01,344
a check for the pipeline.

239
00:18:03,184 --> 00:18:08,448
Let's land this. You should now be able to
say, in your own words: Git records one

240
00:18:08,488 --> 00:18:13,816
permission bit, executable or not, and
stores a symbolic link as a blob holding the

241
00:18:13,856 --> 00:18:18,408
link text. It tracks files, so an
empty directory doesn't exist for it.

242
00:18:18,448 --> 00:18:23,776
On a case-insensitive filesystem, a case-only
rename made with mv never reaches the

243
00:18:23,816 --> 00:18:28,044
index, and two paths that differ
only by case collide on checkout.

244
00:18:28,084 --> 00:18:33,412
I rename with git mv and detect collisions
in CI. Git stores the bytes it is given,

245
00:18:33,452 --> 00:18:34,835
line endings included.

246
00:18:35,175 --> 00:18:41,137
And git clean deletes files that no object
holds, so I run it with -n first, prefer

247
00:18:41,177 --> 00:18:45,476
-X to -x, and give it the
narrowest scope that does the job.

248
00:18:47,316 --> 00:18:54,405
Read sections 4.10 to 4.17 of Chapter
4, and do the Practice section 4.19.

249
00:18:54,745 --> 00:19:00,971
